@* The line that says which spirit is in a seat: the spirit's colour down the leading edge, its
   name, its play style under that, and its elements at the far end. The setup form's seat button
   and the recorded game's seat card are the same seat at two moments, and each drew this line
   itself — which is what the card's own note about "variations of one component" was promising
   and nothing was holding it to.

   No wrapper of its own, because the row differs and its contents don't: one of the two is a
   .field-button and the other a plain flex row, so the caller owns the row and only what goes in
   it is here.

   The name is a fragment rather than a string: on a saved game it is the way into every other
   game played as that spirit, and while the game is being built there is nowhere to go yet. *@

<ColorBar Color="@Color"/>
<div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
    <div class="row-title truncate">@Name</div>
    @if (Detail is not null)
    {
        <div class="row-sub truncate">@Detail.PlayStyle</div>
    }
</div>
@if (Detail is not null)
{
    <ElementBadges Elements="Detail.Elements" Size="sm"/>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The spirit's own colour — <see cref="GameLookups.SpiritColor"/>.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Color { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>What the spirit is called, linked or not. See the note above.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RenderFragment Name { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>The catalogue's entry, for the play style and the elements. Null for a spirit
    /// the catalogue has no entry for, which leaves the line as the colour and the name.</summary>
    [Parameter] public SpiritDetail? Detail { get; set; }
}
